  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nStrong 594 dam ridging remains centered across the Southeast \nwith the axis of the ridge extended north through the Plains. \nLocally, we're parked beneath west-northwest mid- level flow, \nand subtle impulses in the flow combined with mesoscale surface \nfeatures will continue to be the driver of our thunderstorm \ncomplexes over the next 48 hours.\n\nFor the rest of Wednesday, expect that we'll be mostly dry into the \nearly afternoon hours with localized subsidence in the wake of \nmorning convection. By late afternoon and evening, we should see \nreinvigoration of showers and storms possibly driven by two \nfeatures. The first will be a subtle surface trough and weak mid-\nlevel wave tracking through southern Michigan and eventually over \nLake Erie. Putting stock in current CAMs is tough, especially as \nthey continue to struggle even with ongoing convection, but their \nconsensus to fire storms off of that feature combined with a lake \nbreeze will likely be one focus. The other is likely to be a density \ngradient along the outflow/cirrus shield from the morning \nconvection. It has sagged south toward Pittsburgh late this \nmorning, but expectation is it will shift a bit back farther \nnortheast as southwesterly flow establishes. CAMs aren't excited\nabout development along it, but struggle to believe that that's\nreally going to be the case. The environment will be potent; \nmodifying our morning sounding in untapped air suggests dew \npoints with a floor in the mid 70s and afternoon highs in the \nupper 80s to low 90s. A belt of 40-50 knot 500 mb flow will \nsupport effective shear of 30-40 knots and MLCAPE values >3000 \nJ/kg and steep, nearly dry adiabatic mid-level lapse rates. Any \nstorm that develops will have a severe threat. Again, think \ncoverage will be highest north and east of Pittsburgh with most \nlikely timing from 4pm-midnight.\n\nConfidence in future storm complex progression decreases with time \nin this setup as it's highly dependent on poorly modeled preceding \nrounds, but Thursday looks more favorable to see more widespread \ncoverage of storms. The mid-level ridge axis will amplify a bit more \nand gradually slide east which will reorient the mid-level flow more \nnorthwesterly such that we're better in the path of impulses and \nresultant MCSs. The question is when we get the most coverage. Some \nsolutions suggest morning convection again similar to Wednesday \nmorning while others don't and hold off until the afternoon. The \nlatter case would result in more widespread, severe storms in a \nfavorable environment with a 70+% probability of 1000 J/kg SBCAPE \nand 30 knots of shear. If storms do move through in the morning, \nthey will carry a severe risk with them as well, there will just be \nmore question about threats and whether or not they're surface-\nbased. Then the waters will become muddier for the afternoon, so we \njust have to take this setup 12-24 hours at a time.\n\nCome Friday, a cold front that's been meandering well off to our \nnorth finally gets a push to move through and will put an end to the \nunsettled pattern for the weekend. More storms are possible in the \nmorning hours ahead of the front, but we should see a cessation from \nnorth to south in the second half of the day as the front passes \nthrough. It'll be very timing dependent, but there is a scenario in \nwhich most of our activity is done by mid-afternoon. Others suggest \nit lingers a bit, especially south of Pittsburgh, into the evening \nhours. The severe outlook for Friday has appropriately been pulled \nsouth and only left our area south of Pittsburgh in a marginal risk.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nAside from the storms, unseasonably warm conditions continue \nthrough the end of the week with the hottest days being \nWednesday and Thursday. Heat risk will remain in the major \ncategory with pockets of extreme in the valleys and urban areas \nwhere temperatures reach the low to mid 90s and dew points in \nthe 70s resulting in heat indices exceeding 100F. Overnight lows\nWednesday night will only dip into the low 70s. Thus, the Heat \nAdvisory remains in effect through Thursday.\n\nHave some lower confidence that Advisory criteria will be \nreached widespread on Thursday if convection is more widespread \nin coverage, but with how much uncertainty there is, will let \nthe headlines ride as is as areas without convective \ncontamination will heat up efficiently.",
